From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 10:43:29 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] Watch CPU limits from one resident thread instead of one per
 JS entry

Coworkerd v36, depends on v27 CPU limits, v33 memory limits and v35 limit events. Fixed upstream v1.20261012.1. The first enabled limit enforcer starts one process-wide watchdog thread; every JS entry, nested auxiliary entries included, registers with it on entry and unregisters on exit instead of creating and joining a thread of its own. The thread sleeps toward the earliest time an entry needs a check (the earliest moment its CPU deadline can arrive, or the end of a termination grace period) in halving steps whose last step is at most 1 ms, and is woken when a new entry needs an earlier check or a limit state leaves NONE; the memory limit reports its transitions through an observer. A check does what the per-entry thread did: terminate the isolate once the budget is spent or the state left NONE, and after the 100 ms grace write the culprit file if the termination has not taken effect. Deadline arithmetic, termination, events, invalidation, culprit attribution and the memory limit callbacks are unchanged. The thread is named limit-watchdog, blocks signals, and is stopped and joined at exit; without an enabled limit it is never created.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

